{"batchcomplete":true,"query":{"pages":[{"pageid":77986589,"ns":0,"title":"AI slop","revisions":[{"revid":1384670855,"parentid":1384254497,"minor":true,"timestamp":"2026-11-09T13:35:22Z","size":91339,"sha1":"fbb20ea81524ac1eb0157ffa8ee1f96553381d37","slots":{"main":{"contentmodel":"wikitext","contentformat":"text/x-wiki","content":"{{Short description|Low-quality AI-generated digital content}}\n \n{{pp-vandalism|small=yes}}\n{{use dmy dates|date=May 2025}}\n{{multiple images\n| total_width       = 300\n| perrow            = 2/1\n| image1            = Shrimp Jesus example.jpg\n| alt1              = A statue of a man, waist up, submerged underwater and covered in bright orange shrimp. There is a halo of shrimp above his head.\n| image2            = Facebook AI slop, \"Shrimp Jesus\" 2.jpg\n| alt2              = A statue of a man floating high over the ocean. Aside from the head and some of his torso, he is made up of shrimp and ocean plants that blend and meld with each other.\n| image3            = Facebook AI slop, \"Shrimp Jesus\" 1.jpg\n| alt3              = A Crustacean man underwater, with a stone head, neck, and hands. His body and arms are a sort of lobster-crab shell, and he has a dozen long crab legs sprouting from his sides.\n| footer            = AI-generated images of \"Shrimp Jesus\" proliferated on [[Facebook]] in 2024.<ref name=\"Hern-2024\"/><ref>{{cite news |last=Notopoulos |first=Katie |title=Why doesn't Facebook just ban AI slop like Shrimp Jesus? |url=https://www.businessinsider.com/meta-facebook-ban-ai-slop-images-shrimp-jesus-why-2024-6 |access-date=4 October 2024 |work=Business Insider}}</ref><ref name=\"Field-2025\"/>\n}}\n'''AI slop''' is an [[internet slang]] referring to [[digital content]] made with [[generative artificial intelligence]] that is perceived as lacking in effort, quality or meaning, and usually produced in high volume to gain advantage, to earn money, or to deceive people.<ref name=\"Hern-2024\">{{Cite web |last1=Hern |first1=Alex |last2=Milmo|first2=Dan |date=2024-05-19 |title=Spam, junk ... slop? The latest wave of AI behind the 'zombie internet' |url=https://www.theguardian.com/technology/article/2024/may/19/spam-junk-slop-the-latest-wave-of-ai-behind-the-zombie-internet |url-status=live |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20260828052259/https://www.theguardian.com/technology/article/2024/may/19/spam-junk-slop-the-latest-wave-of-ai-behind-the-zombie-internet |archive-date=28 August 2026 |access-date=2024-09-28 |website=[[The Guardian]]|name-list-style=and}}</ref><ref name=\"Read-2024\">{{Cite web |last=Read |first=Max |date=2024-09-25 |title=Drowning in Slop |url=https://nymag.com/intelligencer/article/ai-generated-content-internet-online-slop-spam.html |url-access=subscription |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260307052703/https://nymag.com/intelligencer/article/ai-generated-content-internet-online-slop-spam.html |archive-date=2026-03-07 |access-date=2026-03-12 |website=Intelligencer |language=en}}</ref><ref name=\"NYT-2024\">{{Cite web |date=July 24, 2024 |title=How Long Will A.I.'s 'Slop' Era Last? |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2024/07/24/opinion/ai-annoying-future.html |url-status=live |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20240725072623/https://www.nytimes.com/2024/07/24/opinion/ai-annoying-future.html |archive-date=25 July 2024 |website=The New York Times|last=Wallace-Wells|first=David}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last=Izzo |first=Jack |title=Snopestionary: AI slop, explained |url=https://www.snopes.com/articles/470975/ai-slop-media-literacy/ |access-date=7 November 2025 |date=22 August 2025}}</ref> It is a form of [[synthetic media]] usually linked to the monetization in the [[creator economy]] of social media and [[online advertising]].<ref>{{Cite book |last=Diaz Ruiz |first=Carlos |url=https://www.taylorfrancis.com/books/9781003506676 |title=Market-Oriented Disinformation Research: Digital Advertising, Disinformation and Fake News on Social Media |date=2025-03-14 |publisher=Routledge |isbn=978-1-003-50667-6 |edition=1 |location=London |pages=196–218 |language=en |chapter=Bots Talking to Bots: Synthetic Media, AI-Generated Content, and the \"Dead Internet\" Conspiracy Theory |doi=10.4324/9781003506676-9}}</ref> Coined in the 2020s, the term has a pejorative connotation similar to ''[[Spamming|spam]]''.<ref name=\"Read-2024\"/> \"[[wikt:slop#English|Slop]]\" was selected as the 2025 [[Word of the year|Word of the Year]] by both [[Merriam-Webster]] and the [[American Dialect Society]].<ref>{{Cite web |title=2025 Word of the Year: Slop |url=https://www.merriam-webster.com/wordplay/word-of-the-year |access-date=2025-12-20 |website=merriam-webster.com |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://americandialect.org/2025-word-of-the-year-is-slop/|title=2025 Word of the Year Is \"Slop\"|website=American Dialect Society|date=10 January 2026 |access-date=January 10, 2026}}</ref>\n\nAI slop has been defined as \"digital clutter\", \"filler content prioritizing speed and quantity over substance and quality\",<ref name=\"Hughes-2024\">{{Cite web |last=Hughes |first=Johnny |date=2024-09-03 |title=How Businesses Can Avoid AI Slop |url=https://www.entrepreneur.com/growing-a-business/how-businesses-can-avoid-ai-slop/478715 |access-date=2024-09-28 |website=Entrepreneur |language=en}}</ref> and \"shoddy or unwanted AI content in [[social media]], art, books [and] search results\".<ref name=\"NYT-2024a\"/> Jonathan Gilmore, a philosophy professor at the [[City University of New York]], describes AI slop as having an \"incredibly banal, realistic style\" that is easy for the viewer to process.<ref>{{Cite news |date=2024-06-30 |title=The deluge of bonkers AI art is literally surreal |url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/style/of-interest/2024/06/30/ai-art-facebook-slop-artificial-intelligence/ |access-date=2024-09-29 |newspaper=[[Washington Post]]|language=en}}</ref> The effect of increasing AI slop on the [[World Wide Web|web]] has been likened to the increase in [[entropy]] as driven by the [[Second Law of Thermodynamics]].<ref>{{cite conference| first1=Jonathan P. | last1=Bowen | author-link1=Jonathan Bowen | first2=Susan | last2=Hazan | author-link2=Susan Hazan| title=The Second Law of Cultural-dynamics | book-title=EVA London 2026: Electronic Visualisation and the Arts | publisher=[[British Computer Society|BCS]] | series=eWiC | pages=24–31 | doi=10.14236/ewic/EVA2026.4 | doi-access=free }}</ref>\n\n==Origin of the term==\nAs early [[large language model]]s (LLMs) and image [[diffusion model]]s accelerated the creation of high-volume but low-quality text and images, discussion commenced among journalists and on social platforms for the appropriate term for the influx of material. Terms proposed included \"AI garbage\", \"AI pollution\", and \"AI-generated dross\".<ref name=\"NYT-2024\"/> Early uses of the term \"[[wikt:slop|slop]]\" as a descriptor for low-grade AI material apparently came in reaction to the release of AI image generators in 2022. Its early use has been noted among [[4chan]], [[Hacker News]], and [[YouTube]] commentators as a form of in-group [[slang]].<ref name=\"NYT-2024a\"/>\n\nThe British computer programmer [[Simon Willison]] is credited with being an early champion of the term \"slop\" in the mainstream,<ref name=\"Hern-2024\"/><ref name=\"NYT-2024a\">{{Cite web |date=June 11, 2024 |title=First Came 'Spam.' Now, With A.I., We've Got 'Slop' |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2024/06/11/style/ai-search-slop.html |url-status=live |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20240617000019/https://www.nytimes.com/2024/06/11/style/ai-search-slop.html |archive-date=17 June 2024 |website=The New York Times|last=Hoffman|first=Benjamin}}</ref> having used it on his personal blog in May 2024.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Slop is the new name for unwanted AI-generated content |url=https://simonwillison.net/2024/May/8/slop/ |access-date=2024-10-04 |website=simonwillison.net |language=en-gb|date=May 8, 2024|last=Willison|first=Simon}}</ref> However, he has said it was in use long before he began pushing for the term.<ref name=\"NYT-2024a\"/> The term gained increased popularity in the second quarter of 2024 in part because of [[Google]]'s use of its [[Gemini (chatbot)|Gemini]] AI model to generate responses to search queries,<ref name=\"NYT-2024a\" /> and the large quantities of slop on the internet were widely criticized in media headlines during the fourth quarter of 2024.<ref name=\"Hern-2024\" /><ref name=\"Read-2024\" /> It is also used in compound terms; one such example is \"[[Microslop]]\", used in response to increasing integration of the [[Microsoft Copilot]] chatbot into the [[Microsoft Windows|Windows]] operating system.<ref>{{cite Q|Q138527073|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite Q|Q138527089|url-status=live}}</ref>\n\n== Definitions ==\n{{Quote box\n| width = 22em\n| border = 1px\n| fontsize = 100%\n| salign = center\n| quote = To understand modern slop, you have to think of humans as consuming content in the same way that pigs consume food. The goal of pig slop is to maximize nutrient intake while minimizing cost; the goal of A.I. slop is to maximize time spent consuming content while minimizing cost. There's comedy slop, literary slop, art slop, niche slop, slop for kids, political slop—but the substance of slop always matters less than the fact that you’re looking at it.<ref>{{cite web |last=Whang |first=Oliver |date=2 June 2026 |title=Can You Get Rich Quick Off A.I. Slop? |url=https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2026/06/01/magazine/ai-slop-viral-videos.html |url-access=subscription |website=[[The New York Times Magazine]] |access-date=2 June 2026}}</ref>\n| source = Oliver Whang, ''The New York Times''\n| align = right\n}}\n\nAccording to an academic article by Cody Kommers and five other scholars that was published in January 2026, AI slop has \"so far resisted formal definition.\"<ref name=\"Kommers-2026\">{{Cite journal |last1=Kommers |first1=Cody |last2=Duede |first2=Eamon |last3=Gordon |first3=Julia |last4=Holtzman |first4=Ari |last5=McNulty |first5=Tess |last6=Stewart |first6=Spencer |last7=Thomas |first7=Lindsay |last8=So |first8=Richard Jean |last9=Long |first9=Hoyt |date=2026-01-24 |title=Why Slop Matters |journal=  ACM AI Letters|volume=1 |pages=1–6 |doi=10.1145/3786777 |doi-access=free |arxiv=2601.06060 }}</ref> Although they argue it is impossible to precisely describe a boundary between slop and non-slop, Kommers et al. identify three \"prototypical properties\" that characterise AI slop: superficial competence, asymmetric effort and mass producibility.<ref name=\"Kommers-2026\"/>\n\nBeyond these [[Family resemblance|family resemblances]], there are many different kinds of AI slop. Three main \"dimensions of variance\", or ways in which AI slop can vary, are its instrumental utility (why was it created?), the level of personalization (is it so specific as to only be interesting to one person or a small friend group?) and the level of [[surrealism]], where some AI slop is \"ludicrously implausible\", while other slop is more realistic.<ref name=\"Kommers-2026\"/> Art curator [[Francesco D'Isa]] considers \"AI slop\" to be a misleading term, as it implies AI is inherently worse than artistic mediums such as painting. He sees the majority of human culture as having \"always been slop\", with the canon of art being \"the surviving tip of an immense iceberg of forgotten, derivative, or simply boring creations\".<ref>{{cite web |last=D'Isa |first=Francesco |date=December 1, 2025 |title=The Idea of 'AI Slop' Is Slop |url=https://thephilosophicalsalon.com/the-idea-of-ai-slop-is-slop/ |website=The Philosophical Salon |access-date=6 February 2026}}</ref>\n\n==On social media==\n[[File:AI-generated veteran birthday sign image.png|thumb|alt=A white man wearing military fatigues looks sad while sitting in a wheelchair holding a cardboard sign saying \"Today's my birthday, please like.\" His right leg is wrapped in bandages and his wheelchair is in front of an American flag with missing stars.|An AI-generated image depicting a wounded war veteran in front of an American flag, designed to receive sympathetic interactions from US audiences<ref name=\"Read-2024\"/> <!-- PERHAPS: put prompt in hatnote? this caption is so long! -->]]\n\nAI image and video slop have proliferated on social media in part because it can generate revenue for its creators on [[Facebook]] and [[TikTok]], most notably affecting Facebook. This incentivizes individuals from [[developing countries]] to create images that appeal to audiences in the United States, which attract higher advertising rates.<ref name=\"Koebler-2024\">{{Cite web |last=Koebler |first=Jason |date=2024-08-06 |title=Where Facebook's AI Slop Comes From |url=https://www.404media.co/where-facebooks-ai-slop-comes-from/ |access-date=2024-09-29 |website=404 Media |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last1=Tang |first1=Jiaru |last2=Wikström |first2=Patrik |date=2024-09-19 |title='Side job, self-employed, high-paid': behind the AI slop flooding TikTok and Facebook |url=https://theconversation.com/side-job-self-employed-high-paid-behind-the-ai-slop-flooding-tiktok-and-facebook-237638 |access-date=2024-09-29 |website=The Conversation |language=en-US}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Gault |first=Matthew |date=2024-08-07 |title=Facebook's Twisted Incentives Created Its AI Slop Era |url=https://gizmodo.com/facebooks-twisted-incentives-created-its-ai-slop-era-2000484110 |access-date=2024-09-29 |website=Gizmodo |language=en-US}}</ref> A [[medical student]] in India said he made thousands of dollars each month from low effort, AI-generated images on [[Instagram]] and [[Fanvue]]. After his first attempt to go viral failed, he refined his social media posts to include [[MAGA]] rhetoric to appeal to [[Conservatism in the United States|conservative American]] men, creating the [[influencer]] character \"[[Emily Hart (virtual influencer)|Emily Hart]]\" with AI. He tried the same tactic with a [[politically progressive]] message, but he said it was quickly rejected as \"AI slop\".<ref>{{cite magazine|url=https://www.wired.com/story/ai-generated-maga-girls/|title=This Scammer Used an AI-Generated MAGA Girl to Grift 'Super Dumb' Men|last=Dickson|first=E. J.|magazine=[[Wired (magazine)|Wired]]|date=2026-04-21|access-date=2026-04-27}}</ref>\n\nThe journalist Jason Koebler speculated in an article for [[404 Media]] that the bizarre nature of some of the content may be due to the creators using [[Hindi]], [[Urdu]], and [[Vietnamese language|Vietnamese]] prompts (languages which are underrepresented in the model's [[training data]]), or using erratic [[speech-to-text]] methods to translate their intentions into English.<ref name=\"Koebler-2024\"/> Speaking to ''[[New York (magazine)|New York]]'' magazine, a Kenyan creator of slop images described giving [[ChatGPT]] prompts such as \"WRITE ME 10 PROMPT picture OF JESUS WHICH WILLING BRING HIGH ENGAGEMENT ON FACEBOOK{{sic}}\", and then feeding those created prompts into a [[text-to-image]] AI model such as [[Midjourney]].<ref name=\"Read-2024\" />\n\nAI-generated images of plants and plant care misinformation have proliferated on social media.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2025-05-07 |title=Slop Farmer Boasts About How He Uses AI to Flood Social Media With Garbage to Trick Older Women |url=https://futurism.com/slop-farmer-ai-social-media |access-date=2025-06-26 |website=Futurism|last=Dupré|first=Maggie Harrison}}</ref><ref name=\"Froio-2025\">{{Cite web |last=Froio |first=Nicole |date=2025-06-26 |title=AI is ruining houseplant communities online |url=https://www.theverge.com/ai-artificial-intelligence/691355/ai-is-ruining-houseplant-communities-online |url-status=live |archive-url=https://archive.today/20250626144648/https://www.theverge.com/ai-artificial-intelligence/691355/ai-is-ruining-houseplant-communities-online |archive-date=26 June 2025 |access-date=2025-06-26 |website=The Verge |language=en-US}}</ref> Online retailers have used AI-generated images of flowers to sell seeds of plants that do not actually exist.<ref name=\"Froio-2025\" /> Many online [[houseplant]] communities have banned AI-generated content but struggle to moderate large volumes of content posted by [[Social bot|bots]].<ref name=\"Froio-2025\" /> Facebook spammers have been reported as AI-generating images of [[The Holocaust|Holocaust]] victims with fake stories; in reality there are only a handful of historical photographs taken at [[Auschwitz concentration camp]]. The posters were described as \"slop accounts\", and the [[Auschwitz Memorial]] museum called the images a \"dangerous distortion\".<ref>{{Cite web |date=2025-08-29 |title=BBC reveals web of spammers profiting from AI Holocaust images |url=https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/ckg4xjk1g1xo |url-status=live |archive-url=https://archive.today/20250829110356/https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/ckg4xjk1g1xo |archive-date=29 August 2025 |access-date=2025-11-03 |website=[[BBC News Online|BBC]] |language=en-GB}}</ref> History-focused Facebook groups also have been inundated with AI-generated \"historical\" photos.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Duffy |first=Clare |date=2024-09-03 |title=It's not just you. More weird spam is popping up on Facebook {{!}} CNN Business |url=https://www.cnn.com/2024/09/03/tech/facebook-spam-ai-meta |access-date=2025-11-03 |website=CNN |language=en}}</ref>\n\n''[[wikt:slopper|Slopper]]'', a pejorative slang term derived from \"AI slop\", was coined in 2025 to describe someone who is overly reliant on generative AI tools like ChatGPT.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Webster |first=Kayla |date=July 29, 2025 |title=Why Using ChatGPT at Work Could Hurt Your Reputation |url=https://www.inc.com/kaylawebster/why-using-chatgpt-at-work-could-hurt-your-reputation/91220176 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://archive.today/20250730054352/https://www.inc.com/kaylawebster/why-using-chatgpt-at-work-could-hurt-your-reputation/91220176 |archive-date=30 July 2025 |website=[[Inc. (magazine)|Inc.]]}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |date=2025-08-01 |title=Age-Gating the Internet + Cloudflare Takes On A.I. Scrapers + HatGPT |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2025/08/01/podcasts/hardfork-age-restrictions-cloudflare.html |url-status=live |archive-url=https://archive.today/20250801115716/https://www.nytimes.com/2025/08/01/podcasts/hardfork-age-restrictions-cloudflare.html |archive-date=1 August 2025 |access-date=2025-08-08 |work=[[The New York Times]] |language=en}}</ref> Users on social media have begun using AI-generated images to build massive followings by fooling viewers. This strategy has been popularized for those who are interested in an easy way to get an income online as all that is needed is one post, of the hundreds posted weekly, to gain traction and encourage the quickly made content.<ref name=\"LastWeekTonight-2025\">{{Cite AV media |url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TWpg1RmzAbc |title=AI Slop: Last Week Tonight with John Oliver (HBO) |date=2025-06-22 |last=LastWeekTonight |access-date=2025-11-26 |via=YouTube}}</ref> Some creators are frustrated that their hard work is being stolen by AI-generated content. An artist, Michael Jones,<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.michaeljonescs.com/|title=Home|website=Michael Jones Chains}}</ref> created physical wood carvings of animals using a chainsaw, but the style of the sculptures was taken by and used as a source for AI-generated content, which began to surface with other people beside them, claiming to have made the sculptures themselves. Jones stated that AI-slop is \"a huge issue for carvers all over the world who are sadly missing out on the rightful credit exposure to their work...\"<ref name=\"LastWeekTonight-2025\" />\n\nAccording to a 2025 report by video creation and editing service company Kapwing, [[South Korea]] ranks first worldwide in AI slop consumption. Experts say the phenomenon is driven by the country's rapid embrace of new technology.<ref>{{Cite news |date=2026-02-04 |title=Quick on their feed: What's driving the 'AI slop' consumption surge in South Korea |url=https://www.straitstimes.com/asia/east-asia/quick-on-their-feed-whats-driving-the-ai-slop-consumption-surge-in-south-korea |access-date=2026-02-05 |work=The Straits Times |language=en |issn=0585-3923 |agency=The Korea Herald / Asia News Network}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Ji-hye |first=Shin |date=2025-12-06 |title=Korea ranks No. 1 in global views of 'AI slop' content |url=https://www.koreaherald.com/article/10629996 |access-date=2026-02-05 |website=The Korea Herald |language=en}}</ref> In January 2026, [[YouTube]] CEO [[Neal Mohan]] stated that reducing slop and detecting deepfakes were priorities for YouTube in 2026.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Elias |first=Jennifer |date=2026-01-21 |title=YouTube chief says 'managing AI slop' is a priority for 2026 |url=https://www.cnbc.com/2026/01/21/youtube-chief-says-managing-ai-slop-is-a-priority-for-2026-.html |access-date=2026-02-05 |website=CNBC |language=en}}</ref> A March 2026 investigation by ''[[The New York Times]]'' found that around 40% of videos recommended to children, both on the main platform and on [[YouTube Kids]], appear to be AI slop, often with realistic or ''[[Cocomelon]]''-style visuals. The videos often claim to be educational and are disproportionately about the [[English alphabet]], but they often feature nonsensical and flashy content, such as oddly proportioned horses hatching from eggs.<ref>{{cite news |last=Landymore |first=Frank |title=YouTube Filling With Horrifying AI Slop for Children |url=https://futurism.com/artificial-intelligence/youtube-ai-slop-for-children |work=Futurism |date=10 March 2026}}</ref> Around the same time, ''[[The 74]]'' and ''[[Mother Jones (magazine)|Mother Jones]]'' released a report about children's videos on YouTube that feature disturbing content, contain misinformation such as misspelled words or misidentified objects, or depict imitable behavior that is dangerous.<ref>{{cite news |last=Landymore |first=Frank |title=\"Educational\" YouTube AI Slop Encourages Kids to Play in Traffic |url=https://futurism.com/artificial-intelligence/educational-youtube-ai-slop-play-in-traffic |work=Futurism |date=19 March 2026}}</ref> In March 2026, a [[TikTok]] account centered around an AI-generated parody of the reality show [[Love Island (American TV series)|''Love Island'']] using AI-generated anthropomorphic fruits, called ''[[Fruit Love Island]]'', became one of the fastest-growing accounts in the United States, leading to debate in online communities about monetization of AI-generated content.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2026-03-23 |title=Millions are watching food kiss, date, fight and make up on TikTok. What's 'Fruit Love Island'? |url=https://www.yahoo.com/entertainment/article/millions-are-watching-food-kiss-date-fight-and-make-up-on-tiktok-whats-fruit-love-island-234655567.html |access-date=2026-03-24 |website=Yahoo Entertainment |language=en-US|last=Reilly|first=Kaitlin}}</ref>\n\n==In politics==\n{{See also|Slopaganda}}\n===United States===\n{{Further|AI-generated content in American politics}}[[File:Pope Trump AI by Donald Trump.png|thumb|upright|AI-generated image posted by [[Donald Trump]] depicting himself as pope]]In August 2024, ''[[The Atlantic]]'' noted that AI slop was becoming associated with the [[political right in the United States]], who were using it for [[shitposting]] and [[engagement farming]] on social media, with the technology offering \"cheap, fast, on-demand fodder for content\".<ref>{{Cite web |last=Warzel |first=Charlie |date=2024-08-21 |title=The MAGA Aesthetic Is AI Slop |url=https://www.theatlantic.com/technology/archive/2024/08/trump-posts-ai-image/679540/ |access-date=2024-09-29 |website=The Atlantic |language=en}}</ref>\nAI slop is frequently used in political campaigns in an attempt at gaining attention through [[content farm]]ing.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Warzel |first=Charlie |date=2024-10-21 |title=The Slop Candidate |url=https://www.theatlantic.com/technology/archive/2024/10/donald-trump-mcdonalds/680324/ |access-date=2025-03-23 |website=The Atlantic |language=en}}</ref> In 2025, in the first five months of [[Donald Trump]]'s [[Second presidency of Donald Trump|second tenure]] as [[US president]], Trump posted several AI-generated images of himself on official government social media accounts, such as images of him as the [[pope]] or as a muscular man brandishing a [[lightsaber]].<ref>{{Cite web |last=Gault |first=Matthew |date=2025-05-07 |title=The AI Slop Presidency |url=https://www.404media.co/the-ai-slop-presidency/ |access-date=2025-09-28 |website=404 Media |language=en}}</ref> In August 2024, Trump posted a series of AI-generated images on his [[alt-tech]] social media platform, [[Truth Social]], portraying fans of the pop singer [[Taylor Swift]] in \"Swifties for Trump\" T-shirts, as well as an AI-generated image of Swift appearing to endorse the [[Trump 2024 presidential campaign]]. The images originated from the conservative [[Twitter]] account ''@amuse'', which posted numerous AI slop images leading up to the [[2024 United States elections]] that were shared by other high-profile figures within the Republican Party, such as [[Elon Musk]], who has publicly endorsed generative AI.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Robins-Early |first=Nick |date=2024-08-26 |title=How did Donald Trump end up posting Taylor Swift deepfakes? |url=https://www.theguardian.com/technology/article/2024/aug/24/trump-taylor-swift-deepfakes-ai |access-date=2025-03-23 |work=The Guardian}}</ref> In 2025, ''[[Wired (magazine)|Wired]]'' described Trump as \"The first AI slop President\", noting his frequent use of AI-generated images and videos in public messaging. The magazine highlighted examples such as AI depictions of Trump as a fighter pilot and as a religious figure, arguing that his reliance on low-quality generative content marked a new phase in political communication.<ref>{{Cite magazine |last=Lahut |first=Jake |title=Donald Trump Is the First AI Slop President |url=https://www.wired.com/story/donald-trump-ai-slop-white-house/ |access-date=2025-11-26 |magazine=Wired |language=en-US |issn=1059-1028}}</ref>\n\nIn the aftermath of [[Hurricane Helene]] in 2024, [[Republican Party (United States)|Republican]] social media [[Influencer|influencers]] such as [[Laura Loomer]] circulated an AI-generated image of a young girl holding a puppy in a flood, and used it as evidence of the purported failure of President [[Joe Biden]] to respond to the disaster.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2024-10-04 |title=Gullible Trump Cronies Losing Their Minds Over Fake AI Slop on Twitter |url=https://www.yahoo.com/news/gullible-trump-cronies-losing-minds-131558726.html |access-date=2024-10-06 |website=[[Yahoo News]] |language=en-US|last=Dupré|first=Maggie Harrison}}</ref><ref name=\"Field-2025\">{{cite news |last=Field |first=Matthew |date=1 January 2025 |title=Why the internet is filling up with nonsense 'AI slop' |url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/business/2025/01/01/why-the-internet-is-filling-up-with-nonsense-ai-slop/ |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251008231656/https://www.telegraph.co.uk/business/2025/01/01/why-the-internet-is-filling-up-with-nonsense-ai-slop/ |archive-date=8 October 2025 |access-date=3 January 2025 |work=[[The Daily Telegraph]]}}</ref> The Republican activist [[Amy Kremer]] shared the image while acknowledging it was not genuine.<ref>{{cite web |date=October 8, 2024 |title=Hurricane Helene and the 'Fuck It' Era of AI-Generated Slop |url= https://www.404media.co/hurricane-helene-and-the-fuck-it-era-of-ai-generated-slop/ |last=Koebler |first=Jason |website=404 Media |access-date=October 15, 2024}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |last=Warzel |first=Charlie |date=October 10, 2024 |title=I'm Running Out of Ways to Explain How Bad This Is |url=https://www.theatlantic.com/technology/archive/2024/10/hurricane-milton-conspiracies-misinformation/680221/ |access-date=October 15, 2024 |website=The Atlantic}}</ref> The initial version of the [[Make Our Children Healthy Again Assessment]], a report on children's health issues released by a commission of cabinet members and officials of the Trump administration, and led by [[US Department of Health and Human Services]] Secretary [[Robert F. Kennedy Jr.]], reportedly cited nonexistent and garbled references generated using AI.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Limauro |first=Andrea |date=2025-05-29 |title=White House MAHA report may have garbled science by using AI, experts say |url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/health/2025/05/29/maha-rfk-jr-ai-garble/ |access-date=2025-05-31 |newspaper=The Washington Post}}</ref><ref name=\"DouglasReuters\">{{cite web|last=Douglas|first=Leah|url=https://www.reuters.com/business/healthcare-pharmaceuticals/white-house-report-outline-causes-americas-declining-health-2025-05-22/|title=White House 'MAHA' report calls out food, chemicals impact on children's health |work=Reuters |date=22 May 2025 }}</ref>\n\n[[File:AI-Generated Image depicting Donald Trump as Jesus Christ.jpg|right|thumb|287x287px|Trump posted this image to his Truth Social account on April 12, 2026 ([[Orthodox Easter]]). The post was deleted following backlash.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2026-04-13 |title=Trump deletes post depicting him as Jesus-like figure after backlash |url=https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/c17v8y0z9z2o |access-date=2026-04-13 |website=www.bbc.com |language=en-GB}}</ref>]]\nIn February 2025, Trump shared an AI-generated video on [[Truth Social]] and [[Instagram]] depicting a hypothetical [[Gaza Strip]] after a [[2025 Donald Trump Gaza Strip takeover proposal|Trump takeover]]. The video's creator said it was made as political satire.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Hall |first=Rachel |date=2025-03-06 |title='Trump Gaza' AI video intended as political satire, says creator |url=https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2025/mar/06/trump-gaza-ai-video-intended-as-political-satire-says-creator |access-date=2025-11-05 |work=The Guardian |language=en-GB |issn=0261-3077}}</ref> During the [[Gaza war]], AI-generated media was used to exaggerate support for both sides, and to evoke sympathy using fake images of suffering civilians.<ref>{{Cite web |last1=Eisele |first1=Ines |last2=Steinwehr |first2=Uta |title=Fact check: AI fakes in Israel's war against Hamas – DW – 11/10/2023 |url=https://www.dw.com/en/fact-check-ai-fakes-in-israels-war-against-hamas/a-67367744 |access-date=2025-11-16 |website=dw.com |language=en}}</ref> Because of content restrictions in generative AI, these images and videos rarely depict people wounded in battle, instead focusing on damage to buildings. Fake images of attacks were used to avoid accidentally providing [[Open-source intelligence|intelligence]] to enemies.<ref>{{Cite web |last1=Gault |first1=Matthew |last2=Maiberg |first2=Emanuel |date=2025-06-18 |title=The AI Slop Fight Between Iran and Israel |url=https://www.404media.co/the-ai-slop-fight-between-iran-and-israel/ |archive-url=https://archive.today/20250618131842/https://www.404media.co/the-ai-slop-fight-between-iran-and-israel/ |archive-date=2025-06-18 |access-date=2025-11-16 |website=404 Media |language=en}}</ref>\n\nIn response to the [[No Kings protests (October 2025)|No Kings protests]] in October 2025, Trump posted a video depicting himself flying a fighter jet and releasing feces on crowds of demonstrators, including [[Harry Sisson (influencer)|Harry Sisson]], a [[Democratic Party (United States)|Democratic]] influencer.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2025-10-20 |title=Trump posts AI video showing him dumping on No Kings protesters |url=https://www.nbcnews.com/politics/donald-trump/trump-posts-ai-video-dumping-no-kings-protesters-rcna238521 |access-date=2025-10-21 |website=NBC News |language=en|last=Smith|first=Allan}}</ref> In the midst of disruptions to [[Supplemental Nutrition Assistance Program|food stamp]] distribution during the [[2025 US government shutdown]], anonymous social media users began using [[OpenAI]]'s [[Sora (text-to-video model)|Sora]] AI model to post slop videos of \"[[welfare queen]]s\" complaining, stealing, and rioting in supermarkets; many comments to the videos appeared unaware that they were AI-generated, or acknowledged that they were AI-generated but nonetheless useful in pushing a narrative of widespread [[welfare fraud]].<ref>{{cite web |last=Wilkins |first=Joe |title=Racist Influencers Using OpenAI's Sora to Make it Look Like Poor People Are Selling Food Stamps for Cash |url=https://futurism.com/artificial-intelligence/racist-sora-snap-welfare |website=Futurism |access-date=1 November 2025 |date=31 October 2025}}</ref><ref name=\"GilbertWired\">{{Cite magazine|url=https://www.wired.com/story/disinformation-conspiracy-theories-snap-benefits/|title=No, SNAP Benefits Aren't Mostly Used by Immigrants|first=David|last=Gilbert|magazine=Wired |date=2025-10-30|access-date=2026-05-14}}</ref> In April 2026, on [[Orthodox Easter]], Trump posted a picture of himself depicted as [[Jesus]], drawing widespread criticism from Evangelicals and Catholics, resulting in Trump deleting the post hours later and claiming he believed he was depicted as a doctor.<ref>{{cite web |last=Folk |first=Zachary |date=April 13, 2026 |title=Trump Removes Image Of Himself As Jesus—After Even Some Allies Were Outraged |url=https://www.forbes.com/sites/zacharyfolk/2026/04/13/trump-posts-image-of-himself-as-jesus-and-even-some-allies-are-outraged/ |access-date=April 13, 2026 |website=Forbes.com |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |last1=Coster |first1=Helen |last2=Ax |first2=Joseph |date=April 13, 2026 |title=Trump posts AI image of himself as Jesus-like figure, drawing outrage |url=https://www.reuters.com/business/media-telecom/trump-posts-ai-image-himself-jesus-like-figure-drawing-outrage-2026-04-13/ |access-date=April 13, 2026 |publisher=Reuters |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |last=Moses |first=Claire |date=April 13, 2026 |title=After Criticizing Pope, Trump Posts Image of Himself as a Jesus-Like Figure |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2026/04/13/us/politics/trump-jesus-pope-leo-truth-social-post.html |access-date=April 13, 2026 |work=The New York Times |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |last1=Gooding |first1=Dan |last2=Whisnant |first2=Gabe |date=April 13, 2026 |title=Donald Trump Explains Jesus AI Image Post |url=https://www.newsweek.com/donald-trump-jesus-truth-social-post-doctor-pope-11822035 |access-date=April 13, 2026 |website=Newsweek.com |language=en}}</ref>\n\n===Russia and China===\nA study by the analytics company [[Graphika]] found that the Chinese and Russian governments have been using AI-generated slop as propaganda. This includes the use of \"[[spamouflage]]\" as AI-generated content in Western social media featuring fake influencers, which found to be linked to China. These videos often focused on divisive topics aimed to cause disruption with ulterior motives to the presented content.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Collier |first=Kevin |date=19 November 2025 |title=Large online propaganda campaigns are flooding the internet with 'AI slop,' researchers say |url=https://www.nbcnews.com/tech/security/online-propaganda-campaigns-are-using-ai-slop-researchers-say-rcna244618 |access-date=26 November 2025 |work=NBC News}}</ref>\n\n===Canada===\nOn June 9, 2026, [[New Brunswick]] politician [[Bill Oliver (politician)|Bill Oliver]] gave a speech that included the sentence, \"Here’s a more natural, flowing version of that section that reads like a legislative speech rather than a series of short points,\" which he spoke out loud. He apparently did not notice the error and continued reading the speech. Multiple other apparent AI–user communications were also spoken aloud by him during the speech, including \"Here's a more developed and flowing version of the section\" (in reference to a different section). A video of the incident went viral on social media, was described as AI slop, and sparked controversy and calls for him to resign.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://futurism.com/artificial-intelligence/politician-accidentally-reads-ai-slop-speech|title=Braindead Politician Accidentally Reads AI Slop That Someone Left in His Speech: \"Here’s a More Natural, Flowing Version of That Section That Reads Like a Legislative Speech\"|last=Landymore|first=Frank|date=July 24, 2026|website=Futurism}}</ref>\n\n==In advertising==\n[[File:Coca-Cola AI ad - truck with misspelled logo.png|thumb|Still frame from one of the AI-assisted [[Coca-Cola]] holiday commercials in 2024, with the logo misspelled as \"Coca-Coola\"]]\nIn November 2024, [[the Coca-Cola Company]] used AI to create three commercials as part of [[Coca-Cola#Holiday campaigns|their annual holiday campaign]]. These videos were immediately met with backlash from both casual viewers and artists;<ref>{{Cite news |last=Stedman |first=Alex |date=15 November 2025 |title=Coca-Cola's New AI-Generated Holiday Ad Slammed as 'Soulless' and 'Embarrassing': 'This Is Such Slop' |url=https://www.ign.com/articles/coca-colas-new-ai-generated-holiday-ad-slammed-as-soulless-and-embarrassing-this-is-such-slop |access-date=20 November 2025 |work=IGN}}</ref> animator [[Alex Hirsch]], creator of the television series ''[[Gravity Falls]]'', criticized the company's decision not to employ human artists to create the commercial.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Horvath |first=Bruna |date=18 November 2025 |title=Coca-Cola causes controversy with AI-made ad |url=https://www.nbcnews.com/tech/innovation/coca-cola-causes-controversy-ai-made-ad-rcna180665 |access-date=20 November 2025 |work=NBC News}}</ref> In response to the negative feedback, the company defended their decision to use generative AI, stating that \"Coca-Cola will always remain dedicated to creating the highest level of work at the intersection of human creativity and technology\".<ref>{{Cite news |last=Vadukul |first=Alex |date=20 November 2024 |title=Coca-Cola's Holiday Ads Trade the 'Real Thing' for Generative A.I. |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2024/11/20/style/coca-cola-holiday-ads-ai.html |access-date=20 November 2025 |work=The New York Times}}</ref> Coca-Cola continued to utilize AI-generated commercials for their 2025 holiday campaign.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Vjestica |first=Adam |date=4 November 2025 |title=Coca-Cola's holiday ad is AI slop yet again |url=https://www.theshortcut.com/p/coca-colas-holiday-ad-is-ai-slop-yet-again |access-date=20 November 2025 |website=The Shortcut}}</ref>\n\nDuring the holiday season of 2025, [[McDonald's Netherlands]] released an AI-generated Christmas advertisement titled ''[[It's the Most Terrible Time of the Year]]'', which was met with a large amount of backlash. The advert was seen as cynical, portraying Christmas time as \"the most terrible time of the year\". The company turned off comments on YouTube and later removed the initial upload of the video from public view in response, though reuploads of the original were still public on the site.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Placido |first=Dani Di |title=McDonald's AI-Generated Ad Controversy, Explained |url=https://www.forbes.com/sites/danidiplacido/2025/12/09/mcdonalds-ai-generated-ad-controversy-explained/ |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251213051901/https://www.forbes.com/sites/danidiplacido/2025/12/09/mcdonalds-ai-generated-ad-controversy-explained/ |archive-date=2025-12-13 |access-date=2025-12-28 |work=Forbes |language=en |url-status=live |date=2025-12-09}}</ref>\n\nIn March 2025, [[Paramount Pictures]] was criticized for using AI scripting and narration in an [[Instagram]] video promoting the film ''[[Novocaine (2025 film)|Novocaine]]''.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2025-03-10 |title=Paramount skips voice actors, opting for shockingly bad AI slop in 'Novocaine' film promo |url=https://www.tweaktown.com/news/103797/paramount-skips-voice-actors-opting-for-shockingly-bad-ai-slop-in-novocaine-film-promo/index.html |access-date=2025-03-24 |website=TweakTown |language=en-US}}</ref> The ad uses a robotic [[AI voice]] in a style similar to low-quality AI spam videos produced by content farms. [[A24]] received similar backlash for releasing a series of AI-generated posters for the 2024 film ''[[Civil War (film)|Civil War]]''. One poster appears to depict a group of soldiers in a tank-like raft preparing to fire on a large swan, an image which does not resemble the events of the film.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Maker |first=Paul |date=2025-01-09 |title=AI slop: what Labour, Spotify and Coca-Cola can teach us in 2025 |url=https://www.raconteur.net/technology/ai-slop-flops-2025-oped |access-date=2025-03-24 |website=Raconteur |language=en-GB}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Shankar |first=Bradly |date=2024-04-18 |title=A24's lacklustre Civil War ads are the latest example of AI-generated slop |url=https://mobilesyrup.com/2024/04/18/a24-ai-generated-ads-criticism-civil-war/ |access-date=2025-03-24 |website=MobileSyrup |language=en}}</ref>\n\n[[File:McDonald's AI Advert.png|thumb|A still frame from ''[[It's the Most Terrible Time of the Year]]'', a [[McDonald's]] advertisement generated by AI]]\nIn the same month, [[Activision]] posted various advertisements and posters for fake video games such as \"[[Guitar Hero]] Mobile\", \"[[Crash Bandicoot]]: Brawl\", and \"[[Call of Duty]]: Zombie Defender\" that were all made using generative AI on platforms such as [[Facebook]] and Instagram, which many labelled as AI slop.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Phillips |first=Tom |date=2025-03-03 |title=Activision accused of \"AI slop\" yet again, this time for new Guitar Hero |url=https://www.eurogamer.net/activision-accused-of-ai-slop-yet-again-this-time-for-new-guitar-hero |access-date=2025-03-27 |website=Eurogamer.net |language=en}}</ref> The intention of the posts was later stated to act as a survey for interest in possible titles by the company.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Phillips |first=Tom |date=2025-03-04 |title=Activision \"AI slop\" Guitar Hero advert is market research for a fake game that doesn't exist and maybe never will |url=https://www.eurogamer.net/activision-ai-slop-guitar-hero-advert-is-market-research-for-a-fake-game-that-doesnt-exist-and-maybe-never-will |access-date=2025-03-27 |website=Eurogamer.net |language=en}}</ref> The [[Italian brainrot]] AI trend was widely adopted by advertisers as an attempt to adjust to younger audiences.<ref>{{Cite web |date=15 April 2025 |title=Why Brands Are Embracing 'Italian Brainrot' to Go Viral on TikTok and Win Over Gen Z |url=https://www.news10.com/business/press-releases/ein-presswire/803101615/why-brands-are-embracing-italian-brainrot-to-go-viral-on-tiktok-and-win-over-gen-z/ |website=News 10 ABC |access-date=29 April 2025 |archive-date=29 June 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250629062109/https://www.news10.com/business/press-releases/ein-presswire/803101615/why-brands-are-embracing-italian-brainrot-to-go-viral-on-tiktok-and-win-over-gen-z/ |url-status=dead }}</ref>\n\nDuring [[Super Bowl LX]] in the United States in 2026, the vodka brand [[Svedka]] aired an AI-generated commercial featuring two robots, apparently male and female, drinking Svedka while dancing at a club.<ref>{{cite web | title=Svedka Super Bowl commercial powered by AI with dancing robots | url=https://admeter.usatoday.com/story/sports/ad-meter/2026/02/08/svedka-super-bowl-commercial-2026-ai-robots/88527198007/ }}</ref> The ad was met with heavy criticism from Super Bowl viewers, and many took to social media to express their disdain.<ref>{{cite web |last=Tan |first=Nicholas |title=Weird Svedka Vodka AI Super Bowl Ad Gives Viewers Temu I, Robot Vibes |url=https://www.mandatory.com/culture/1727739-svedka-vodka-super-bowl-ai-ad-reactions |website=Mandatory |access-date=14 March 2026 |language=en |date=9 February 2026}}</ref> Svedka claimed that the ad was a collaboration between AI and human creativity, as the AI was trained on a TikTok video that won an open call for dance routines.<ref>{{cite web |last=Kiefer |first=Brittaney |title=Creatives React: Svedka's Forgettable Super Bowl Ad Proves AI Isn't an Idea |url=https://www.adweek.com/creativity/creatives-react-svedkas-forgettable-super-bowl-ad-proves-ai-isnt-an-idea/ |website=Adweek |access-date=14 March 2026 |date=8 February 2026}}</ref> In March 2026, researchers at the cybersecurity firm DoubleVerify identified a network of more than 200 websites publishing AI-generated content. The operation, called \"AutoBait\", used templated prompts in a large language model to produce articles and images designed primarily to generate advertising revenue.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Sabin |first=Sam |date=2026-03-04 |title=Inside a network of more than 200 AI slop websites gaming advertisers |url=https://www.axios.com/2026/03/04/ai-slop-autobait-network-fraudsters-doubleverify |access-date=2026-03-07 |website=Axios |language=en}}</ref>\n\n[[File:A pastry stall using AIGC image on its menu in Shanghai, China 20260912 (cropped).jpg|thumb|A food stall using AI-generated images on its menu in Shanghai, China]]\nSome restaurants have used AI-generated images in their menus, with most people calling the images uncanny or unappetizing.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Bonos |first1=Lisa |title=Uncanny and unappetizing: appetites spoil as AI images take over food menus |url=https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2026/sep/06/ai-food-menu-images |access-date=13 September 2026 |work=The Guardian |date=2026-09-06}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Wile |first1=Rob |title=Side of slop? Bizarre AI food images jump from online to IRL menus |url=https://www.nbcnews.com/tech/tech-news/side-slop-bizarre-ai-food-images-jump-online-irl-menus-rcna594786 |access-date=13 September 2026 |work=NBC News |date=2026-09-02}}</ref>\n\n==In event listings==\n[[File:Willy's Chocolate Experience advertisement.png|thumb|alt=A detailed but poorly-done illustration a man surrounded by candy, themed vaguely after Roald Dahl's Willy Wonka. Text above and below are misspelled or nonsense. Shapes morph and blend into one another.|One of the [[Willy's Chocolate Experience]] advertisements, with uncorrected spelling errors, nonsensical words, and bizarre illustrations]]\n\nFantastical promotional graphics for the 2024 [[Willy's Chocolate Experience]] event, which took place in [[Glasgow]], characterized as \"AI-generated slop\",<ref>{{cite news |last=Currie |first=Richard |title=Willy Wonka event leaves bitter taste with artificially sweetened promises |url=https://www.theregister.com/2024/02/28/wonka_ai_hellhole/ |access-date=4 November 2024 |work=The Register |date=28 February 2024}}</ref> misled audiences into attending an event that was held in a sparsely decorated warehouse. Tickets were marketed through [[Facebook]] advertisements showing AI-generated imagery, with no genuine photographs of the venue.<ref>{{cite magazine |last=Murphy |first=Chris |title=\"Willy's Chocolate Experience\" Nightmare: What Went Wrong? |url=https://www.vanityfair.com/hollywood/willys-chocolate-experience-nightmare-what-went-wrong |access-date=4 November 2024 |magazine=Vanity Fair |date=29 February 2024}}</ref>\n\nIn October 2024, thousands of people were reported to have assembled for a non-existent Halloween parade in [[Dublin]] as a result of a listing on an aggregation listings website, MySpiritHalloween.com, which used AI-generated content.<ref>{{cite news |last=Davis |first=Barney |date=1 November 2024 |title=Chaos in Dublin as thousands turn up for AI 'hoax' Halloween parade that didn't exist |url=https://www.independent.co.uk/news/world/europe/dublin-fake-halloween-parade-ireland-ai-advert-b2639505.html |access-date=1 November 2024 |work=The Independent |language=en}}</ref><ref name=\"NYT-2024b\">{{Cite news |date=November 1, 2024 |title=All Trick, No Treat: Dublin Crowds Turn Up for Halloween Parade That Wasn't |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2024/11/01/world/europe/fake-halloween-parade-dublin.html |work=The New York Times|last1=Moses|first1=Claire|last2=Mather|first2=Victor|name-list-style=and}}</ref> The listing went viral on TikTok and [[Instagram]].<ref>{{cite news |last=Fleck |first=Holly |title=Dublin: Halloween parade listing 'mistake' says website owner |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/czd5319pz2ro |access-date=14 November 2024 |work=BBC News |date=2 November 2024}}</ref> A similar parade had previously been held in [[Galway]]. Dublin had hosted parades in prior years, although there was no parade in 2024.<ref name=\"NYT-2024b\"/> One analyst characterized the website, which appeared to use AI-generated staff pictures, as likely using AI \"to create content quickly and cheaply where opportunities are found\".<ref>{{cite news |last=O'Shea |first=Kerry |date=31 October 2024 |title=All trick no treat – \"Hoax\" Halloween parade draws big crowds to Dublin's O'Connell St |url=https://www.irishcentral.com/news/dublin-halloween-parade |access-date=1 November 2024 |work=IrishCentral.com |language=en}}</ref> The site's owner said, \"We asked ChatGPT to write the article for us, but it wasn't ChatGPT by itself.\" In the past the site had removed non-existent events when contacted by their venues, but in the case of the Dublin parade the site owner said that \"no one reported that this one wasn't going to happen\". MySpiritHalloween.com updated their page to say that the parade had been \"canceled\" when they became aware of the issue.<ref>{{Cite magazine |last=Knibbs |first=Kate |title=The Guy Behind the Fake AI Halloween Parade Listing Says You've Got It All Wrong |url=https://www.wired.com/story/ai-halloween-parade-listing-dublin-interview/ |access-date=2024-11-03 |magazine=Wired |issn=1059-1028}}</ref>\n\n==In books==\nOnline booksellers and library vendors now have many titles that are written by AI and are not curated into collections by librarians. The digital media provider [[Hoopla (digital media service)|Hoopla]], which supplies libraries with [[ebook]]s and downloadable content, has generative AI books with fictional authors and dubious quality, which cost libraries money when checked out by unsuspecting patrons.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Maiberg |first=Emanuel |date=2025-02-04 |title=AI-Generated Slop Is Already In Your Public Library |url=https://www.404media.co/ai-generated-slop-is-already-in-your-public-library-3/ |access-date=2025-02-06 |website=404 Media |language=en}}</ref> \n\nUsers of Amazon [[Kindle Store|Kindle]] and other [[ebook]] sites have reported concerns with the increased output of novels seemingly created artificially. Author Jane Friedman stated she had to report 29 novels in one week which were created by AI but used her name and likeness. Kindle created a limit of three novels per day from a single author to combat the issue.<ref>{{Cite web |title=The AI-generated Books Trend is Getting Worse |url=https://aibusiness.com/responsible-ai/the-ai-generated-books-phenomenon-is-getting-worse |access-date=2025-11-04 |website=AI Business |language=en}}</ref> \n\nOn Amazon, there are many AI-generated [[Mushroom hunting|mushroom foraging]] books being sold.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2023-11-06 |title=AI generated mushroom books – life and death |url=https://www.newyorkmyc.org/ai-generated-mushroom-books-life-and-death/ |access-date=2026-04-21 |language=en-US}}</ref> These books often contain inaccurate and distorted AI-generated images of mushrooms,<ref>{{Cite news |last=Grady |first=Constance |date=2024-04-29 |title=The AI grift that can literally poison you |url=https://www.vox.com/24141648/ai-ebook-grift-mushroom-foraging-mycological-society |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250330162845/https://www.vox.com/24141648/ai-ebook-grift-mushroom-foraging-mycological-society |archive-date=2025-03-30 |access-date=2026-04-21 |work=Vox |language=en-US}}</ref> as well as text likely written by ChatGPT. Many of these books include fake, AI-generated authors as well.<ref name=\"Cole-2023\">{{Cite web |last=Cole |first=Samantha |date=2023-08-29 |title='Life or Death:' AI-Generated Mushroom Foraging Books Are All Over Amazon |url=https://www.404media.co/ai-generated-mushroom-foraging-books-amazon/ |access-date=2026-04-21 |website=404 Media |language=en}}</ref> Experts warn that, due to the risk of [[mushroom poisoning]], this is extremely dangerous.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Milmo |first=Dan |date=2023-09-01 |title=Mushroom pickers urged to avoid foraging books on Amazon that appear to be written by AI |url=https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2023/sep/01/mushroom-pickers-urged-to-avoid-foraging-books-on-amazon-that-appear-to-be-written-by-ai |access-date=2026-04-21 |work=The Guardian |language=en-GB |issn=0261-3077}}</ref> In 2023, the [[New York Mycological Society]] issued a statement about AI-generated mushroom books on social media, stating that misidentification could \"literally mean life or death\".<ref name=\"Cole-2023\" /> \n\nIn February 2023, the science-fiction magazine ''[[Clarkesworld Magazine|Clarkesworld]]'' had to temporarily close short story submissions after receiving massive amounts of AI spam, which editor Neil Clarke attributed to people from outside the speculative fiction community trying to make easy money.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Edwards |first=Benj |date=2023-02-21 |title=Sci-fi becomes real as renowned magazine closes submissions due to AI writers |url=https://arstechnica.com/information-technology/2023/02/sci-fi-becomes-real-as-renowned-magazine-closes-submissions-due-to-ai-writers/ |access-date=2025-11-16 |website=Ars Technica |language=en}}</ref> Clarke expressed worry that this trend would result in higher barriers of entry for new authors.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Clarke |first=Neil |title=A Concerning Trend – Neil Clarke |url=https://neil-clarke.com/a-concerning-trend/ |access-date=2025-11-16 |language=en-US}}</ref> LLMs trained on similar data often produce similar-sounding stories, leading to the phenomenon of a lighthouse keeper named Elias Thorne, who shows up in a disproportionate number of stories generated by multiple LLMs.  When this slop floods Amazon and LLMs are trained on this new data, it can cause [[model collapse]] and reinforce stories featuring Thorne as a lighthouse keeper.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/2026/jun/17/elias-thorne-ai-generated-stories|title=The curious case of Elias Thorne – and what he tells us about AI inbreeding|last=Mahdawi|first=Arwa|work=[[The Guardian]]|date=2026-06-17|accessdate=2026-06-17}}</ref>\n\n\"Non-fiction\" biographies have become prominent on Amazon, often containing fabrications and inaccuracies.<ref>{{cite web |last1=Hill |first1=Kashmir |title=I Got Slopped |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2026/07/16/technology/ai-slop-books-biography-amazon.html |website=The New York Times |date=16 July 2026 }}</ref> During elections in various countries, inaccurate AI written biographies of politicians have been noted to appear, for instance during the [[2026 Scottish Parliament election]] one such book claimed that [[John Swinney]] was born in the United States to Polish immigrant parents. A 2024 [[Canadian Broadcasting Corporation|CBC]] poll found that 18% of voters in Alberta and Saskatchewan mistake such books as legitimate works.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Kozlowski |first=Michael |date=2025-07-18 |title=Amazon is battling AI generated political biographies - Good e-Reader |url=https://goodereader.com/blog/e-book-news/amazon-is-battling-ai-generated-political-biographies |access-date=2026-10-27 |website=goodereader.com |language=en-CA}}</ref>\n\n== In video games ==\n''[[Call of Duty: Black Ops 6]]'' includes assets generated by AI. Since the game's initial release, many players had accused [[Treyarch]] and [[Raven Software]] of using AI to create in-game assets, including loading screens, emblems, and calling cards. A particular example was a loading screen for the zombies game mode that depicted \"Necroclaus\", a zombified [[Santa Claus]] with six fingers on one hand, among other irregularities.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Yin-Poole |first=Wesley |date=2024-12-09 |title=Call of Duty Fans Give Black Ops 6's Zombie Santa Loading Screen the Finger Amid 'AI Slop' Backlash |url=https://www.ign.com/articles/call-of-duty-fans-give-black-ops-6s-zombie-santa-loading-screen-the-finger-amid-ai-slop-backlash |access-date=2025-02-26 |website=IGN |language=en}}</ref> The previous entry in the franchise, ''[[Call of Duty: Modern Warfare III (2023 video game)|Call of Duty: Modern Warfare III]]'', was also accused of selling AI-generated [[Microtransaction|cosmetics]].<ref>{{Cite web |last=Yin-Poole |first=Wesley |date=2024-07-24 |title=Activision Reportedly Sold an AI-Generated Call of Duty: Modern Warfare 3 Cosmetic |url=https://www.ign.com/articles/activision-reportedly-sold-an-ai-generated-call-of-duty-modern-warfare-3-cosmetic |access-date=2025-02-26 |website=IGN |language=en}}</ref> In February 2025, Activision disclosed ''Black Ops 6''{{'}}s usage of generative AI to comply with [[Valve Corporation|Valve]]'s policies on AI-generated or assisted products on [[Steam (service)|Steam]]. Activision states on the game's product page on Steam that \"Our team uses generative AI tools to help develop some in game assets.\"<ref>{{Cite web |date=2025-02-25 |title=Call Of Duty Discloses AI Slop After Months Of Players Complaining |url=https://kotaku.com/black-ops-6-ai-calling-card-loading-screen-blops-6-cod-1851766313 |access-date=2025-02-26 |website=Kotaku |language=en}}</ref> ''[[Call of Duty: Black Ops 7|Call of Duty: Black Ops]]'' continued the usage of AI-generated content with [[Studio Ghibli]]-styled calling cards, which have been criticized by fans.<ref>{{Cite web |last=White |first=Lewis |date=14 November 2025 |title=Call of Duty: Black Ops 7 is littered with AI art slop, because your $70 means nothing anymore |url=https://frvr.com/blog/call-of-duty-black-ops-7-is-littered-with-ai-art-slop-because-your-70-means-nothing-anymore/ |access-date=20 November 2025 |website=FRVR}}</ref>\n\nIn 2024, [[Rovio Entertainment]] released a demo of a mobile game called ''Angry Birds: Block Quest'' on [[Android (operating system)|Android]]. The game featured AI-generated images for loading screens and backgrounds.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Kalita |first=Parash Jyoti |date=2024-11-01 |title=Angry Birds Block Quest: Rovio's latest title in the franchise has soft-launched for Android in the US |url=https://gamingonphone.com/news/angry-birds-block-quest-has-soft-launched-for-android-in-the-us/ |access-date=2025-05-09 |website=GamingonPhone |language=en-US}}</ref> It was heavily criticized by players, who called it [[shovelware]] and disapproved of Rovio's use of AI images.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2024-11-02 |title=Angry Birds Block Quest Soft Launches on Android, Sparking Debate Over Generative AI Content – |url=https://games.mxdwn.com/news/angry-birds-block-quest-soft-launches-on-android-sparking-debate-over-generative-ai-content/ |access-date=2025-05-09 |website=mxdwn Games |language=en-US}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=2024-11-01 |title=Angry Birds Block Quest – Soft launch for Android in the US |url=https://daikama.com/news/angry-birds-block-quest-soft-launch-for-android-in-the-us/ |access-date=2025-05-09 |language=en-US|website=daikama.com}}</ref> It was eventually discontinued and removed from the [[Google Play|Play Store]]. According to a 2025 report by AI and Games, about 20% of games published on Steam in 2025 include an \"AI disclosure\", indicating they use generative AI tools for some assets. The same report uses explicitly the term \"AI shovelware problem\", arguing that many of these games rely heavily on AI-generated visuals (textures, images, dialogue, story bits, etc.), often with mixed or poor quality.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Thompson |first=Tommy |title=A Failure of Platform Policy: The AI Shovelware Problem {{!}} 23/07/25 |url=https://www.aiandgames.com/p/a-failure-of-platform-policy-the |access-date=2025-11-26 |website=aiandgames.com |date=23 July 2025 |language=en}}</ref>\n\nSome [[indie game]] developers commented that there were attempts at copying footage or [[game demo]]s of indie games with AI, in some cases being released to market before the original games have been.<ref>{{cite news |last=Orland |first=Kyle |title=AI game clones are making indie developers afraid to show their work |url=https://arstechnica.com/gaming/2026/10/ai-game-clones-are-making-indie-developers-afraid-to-show-their-work/ |access-date=3 November 2026 |work=[[Ars Technica]] |date=19 October 2026 |language=en}}</ref> Alliance Arts cancelled releasing public demos of ''Drunken Goddess Reflux '' due to worries over having its work copied by AI.<ref>{{cite news |last=Lane |first=Rick |title=Developer cancels public demo of its upcoming horror drinking game, fearing it will be instantly cloned by AI vibe coders: 'We sincerely apologise for the short notice' |url=https://www.pcgamer.com/gaming-industry/game-development/developer-cancels-public-demo-of-its-upcoming-horror-drinking-game-fearing-it-will-be-instantly-cloned-by-ai-vibe-coders-we-sincerely-apologise-for-the-short-notice/ |access-date=3 November 2026 |work=[[PC Gamer]] |date=17 October 2026 |language=en}}</ref>\n\n== In film and television ==\n[[File:Late Night with the Devil AI image.jpg|thumb|AI-generated image in a screenshot from ''Late Night with the Devil'']]\nSome films have received backlash for including AI-generated content. The film ''[[Late Night with the Devil]]'' was criticized by some reviewers for its use of AI-generated images.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2024-04-19 |title=Shudder Just Quietly Released the Most Controversial Thriller of the Year |url=https://www.inverse.com/entertainment/late-night-with-the-devil-shudder-streaming-ai-art |access-date=2025-03-24 |website=Inverse |language=en|last=Kleinman|first=Jake}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=2024-11-20 |title=Late Night with the Devil: The Horror Film with Horrifying Implications |url=https://shadow-magazine.com/2024/11/20/late-night-with-the-devil-the-horror-film-with-horrifying-implications/ |access-date=2025-03-24 |website=Shadow Magazine |language=en-GB |archive-date=24 March 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250324214122/https://shadow-magazine.com/2024/11/20/late-night-with-the-devil-the-horror-film-with-horrifying-implications/ |url-status=dead |last=Carson|first=Anna}}</ref> Several AI-generated images were used as interstitial title cards, with one image featuring a skeleton with inaccurate bone structure and fingers that appear disconnected from its hands.<ref>{{Cite journal |last=Hüsmen |first=Arife |date=November 2024 |title=AI in the Creative Industries: Strikes, Debates and Implications |url=https://www.researchgate.net/publication/387368228 |journal=20th International Symposium of Communication in the Millennium |pages=193–199 |via=ResearchGate}}</ref>\n\nSome streaming services, such as [[Amazon Prime Video]], have used AI to generate slop posters and thumbnail images. A low-quality AI poster was used for the 1922 film ''[[Nosferatu]]'', depicting [[Count Orlok]] in a way that does not resemble his look in the film.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2025-01-07 |title=Amazon Mocked for Slapping AI-Generated Poster on Beloved 1922 Film \"Nosferatu\" |url=https://futurism.com/amazon-ai-generated-movie-poster-nosferatu |access-date=2025-03-24 |website=Futurism}}</ref> A thumbnail image for ''[[12 Angry Men (1957 film)|12 Angry Men]]'' on [[Amazon Freevee]] used AI to depict 19 men with smudged faces, none of whom appeared to bear any similarities to the characters in the film.<ref>{{Cite web |last=John |first=Daniel |date=2024-06-12 |title=Not even Amazon is safe from the rise of AI 'slop' |url=https://www.creativebloq.com/news/amazon-ai-slop |access-date=2025-03-24 |website=Creative Bloq |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=The mystery of Amazon Freevee's A.I.-generated 12 Angry Men poster |url=https://www.avclub.com/amazon-freevee-12-angry-men-artificial-intelligence-1851523558 |access-date=2025-03-24 |website=AV Club |language=en-US|date=2024-06-06|last=Carr|first=Mary Kate}}</ref> Additionally, some viewers have noticed that many plot descriptions appear to be generated by AI, which some people have characterized as slop. One synopsis briefly listed on the site for the film ''[[Dog Day Afternoon]]'' read: \"A man takes hostages at a bank in Brooklyn. Unfortunately I do not have enough information to summarize further within the provided guidelines.\"<ref>{{Cite web |date=2025-02-19 |title=Amazon's Prime Video Appears to Have Left AI Slop in the Public Description of an Oscar-Winning Al Pacino Film |url=https://futurism.com/amazon-video-ai-slop-description-al-pacino-film |access-date=2025-03-24 |website=Futurism}}</ref> Amazon Prime Video published a recap of season one of the dystopian sci-fi TV show ''[[Fallout (American TV series)|Fallout]]''. It was removed after audiences noticed mistakes, including claims that a scene set in 2077 was set in the 1950s.<ref>{{Cite web |last=McMahon |first=Liv |date=2025-12-12 |title=Amazon pulls AI recap from Fallout TV show after it made several mistakes |url=https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/c3r77j5nze5o |access-date=2026-03-18 |website=bbc.com |language=en-GB}}</ref>\n\nIn one case, [[Deutsche Telekom]] removed a series from their media offerings after viewers complained about the bad quality and monotonous German voice dubbing (translated from original Polish) and it was found out that it was done via AI.<ref>{{cite web |title='Murderesses': MagentaTV entfernt Serie mit KI-Synchronisation |url=https://www.heise.de/news/Murderesses-MagentaTV-entfernt-Serie-mit-KI-Synchronisation-10269372.html |website=Heise Online |access-date=25 April 2025 |language=de |date=4 February 2025}}</ref>{{better source needed|date=August 2026|reason=I don't think the source explicitly describes the material as \"AI slop\"}}\n\nIn March 2025, [[Carsey-Werner]] upscaled ''[[Roseanne (TV series)|Roseanne]]'' and ''[[A Different World]]'' using generative AI. This process distorted text in the background, rendering it illegible.<ref>{{cite web|title=Corporate Hacks Have Turned 'Roseanne' and 'A Different World' Into AI-Upscaled Nightmares|url=https://gizmodo.com/corporate-hacks-have-turned-roseanne-and-a-different-world-into-ai-upscaled-nightmares-2000572523|website=Gizmodo|access-date=22 May 2026|date=6 March 2025}}</ref> The 2026 web series ''[[On This Day... 1776]]'', produced by [[Darren Aronofsky]], was created with AI generated visuals and [[Screen Actors Guild]] [[voice actor]]s. It was widely panned by critics with many calling it \"slop\". In March 2026, [[WildBrain]] had upconverted various series with an AI upscaler, including ''[[The Super Mario Bros. Super Show!]]''. When it aired on [[MeTV Toons]], viewers found that, among other problems, the upscaling model had not handled text properly, thus making the opening title read \"Suele Mario Bros\" instead.<ref>{{cite web|title=They Brought Back The ''Super Mario Bros.'' Cartoon And Sloppified It With AI|url=https://kotaku.com/they-brought-back-the-super-mario-bros-cartoon-back-and-sloppified-it-with-ai-2000683558|website=Kotaku|access-date=22 May 2026|date=31 March 2026}}</ref>\n\n==In music==\n{{see also|Music and artificial intelligence|Virtual band}}\n\nLarge volumes of AI-generated tracks have been uploaded with the aim of manipulating streaming platform royalties. For example, in September 2024 Michael Smith of North Carolina was charged with [[wire fraud]] conspiracy after using hundreds of thousands of AI-generated songs and bots to generate more than US$10 million in royalties.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2024-09-04 |title=Southern District of New York {{!}} North Carolina Musician Charged With Music Streaming Fraud Aided By Artificial Intelligence {{!}} United States Department of Justice |url=https://www.justice.gov/usao-sdny/pr/north-carolina-musician-charged-music-streaming-fraud-aided-artificial-intelligence |access-date=2025-11-13 |website=justice.gov |language=en}}</ref> In June 2025, [[Deezer]] estimated that as much as 70% of streams of AI-generated tracks on its platform were fraudulent, highlighting concerns about mass low-quality output competing with human-made music.<ref>{{Cite news |date=2025-06-18 |title=Up to 70% of streams of AI-generated music on Deezer are fraudulent, says report |url=https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2025/jun/18/up-to-70-of-streams-of-ai-generated-music-on-deezer-are-fraudulent-says-report |access-date=2025-11-13 |work=The Guardian |language=en-GB |issn=0261-3077}}</ref> \n\nIn April 2024, [[Pink Floyd]] held a fan-made music video contest for tracks from the ''[[The Dark Side of the Moon|Dark Side of the Moon]]'' album to celebrate the band's 50th anniversary. Among ten chosen entries, the music video for the track \"[[Any Colour You Like]]\" was noted for being made using the [[Stable Diffusion]] software, prompting online backlash from fans against the band and the video's submitter for its AI-generated nature as well as its perceived low quality.<ref>{{Cite magazine |last=Stenzel |first=Wesley |date=2024-04-08 |title=AI-generated entry wins Pink Floyd's 'Dark Side of the Moon' video competition |url=https://ew.com/ai-wins-pink-floyd-s-dark-side-of-the-moon-video-competition-8628712 |access-date=2026-01-13 |magazine=[[Entertainment Weekly]] |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Kelsey |first=Nagel |date=2024-04-09 |title=Fan Fury Over AI-Generated Video Winning Pink Floyd's Animation Competition |url=https://www.96fm.com.au/entertainment/music/fans-are-furious-over-an-ai-generated-video-winning-pink-floyds-dsotm-animation-competition/ |access-date=2026-01-13 |website=[[Gold 96FM]] |language=en-US}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Growcoot |first=Matt |date=2024-04-09 |title=Fury as AI Video Wins Competition Held by Pink Floyd |url=https://petapixel.com/2024/04/09/fury-as-ai-video-wins-competition-held-by-pink-floyd/ |access-date=2026-01-13 |website=[[PetaPixel]] |language=en}}</ref>\n\nIn November 2024, [[Kanye West]] released an unexpected music video for the song \"Bomb\" from ''[[Vultures 2]]'', featuring AI-generated versions of his daughters, [[North West (musician)|North West]] and Chicago West. The video shows the children racing through a desert in futuristic [[Cybertruck]]-like vehicles, with both providing vocals—North partly in Japanese and Chicago in a freestyle style. The release drew widespread debate, with many fans criticizing West's increasing reliance on AI and specifically referring to the project as \"AI slop\", expressing discomfort with the use of AI replicas of his children.<ref>{{Cite web |last=West |first=Alex |date=2024-11-28 |title=Kanye West drops 'AI slop' music video featuring daughters North and Chicago |url=https://www.themirror.com/entertainment/breaking-kanye-west-drops-ai-829497 |access-date=2025-11-20 |website=The Mirror US |language=en}}</ref>\n\nIn July 2025, numerous media outlets reported on accusations that an indie band named '''the Velvet Sundown'''<!-- the band name is bolded per [[MOS:BOLDREDIRECT]], since its name redirects here -->, which in only a few weeks had amassed over 850,000 listeners on [[Spotify]], was AI-generated. Critics observed that there were no records of any performances by the band, that individual band members had no social media presence, and that their promotional images appeared to be fake.<ref name=\"BBC04072025\">{{Cite web |date=2025-07-04 |title=AI claims and a hoax spokesman: Viral band confuses the world of music |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cp8mjnn7eqno |access-date=2025-07-04 |website=BBC News}}</ref> [[Deezer]]'s AI detection tool flagged the band's music as being 100% AI-generated.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2025-07-04 |title=The Velvet Sundown: Everything we know about the band accused of being AI|url=https://www.independent.co.uk/arts-entertainment/music/news/the-velvet-sundown-ai-band-b2782755.html|access-date=2025-07-04|website=The Independent}}</ref> ''[[Rolling Stone]]'' (which described the Velvet Sundown as \"obviously fictional\") had reported that a spokesperson for the band named Andrew Frelon had admitted that their music was an \"art hoax\" generated using the AI tool [[Suno AI|Suno]]. However, Frelon later stated that his statement was itself a hoax and that he had no connection to the band.<ref name=\"BBC04072025\"/><ref>{{Cite magazine |date=2025-07-04 |title='Spokesperson' for AI 'Band' Velvet Sundown Now Says He Is an Imposter |url=https://www.rollingstone.com/music/music-features/velvet-sundown-ai-band-suno-1235377652/ |magazine=Rolling Stone|last1=Browne|first1=David|first2=Brian|last2=Hiatt}}</ref> However, within a week, the Velvet Sundown's artist biography on Spotify had been updated to say that the band was \"a synthetic music project guided by human creative direction, and composed, voiced, and visualized with the support of artificial intelligence\", intended as \"artistic provocation\".<ref>{{Cite web |date=2025-07-08 |title='Not quite human': Popular band confirmed to have been AI, stunning fans |url=https://www.jpost.com/business-and-innovation/all-news/article-860322|access-date=2025-07-08|website=Jerusalem Post|last=Greyman-Kennard|first=Danielle}}</ref><ref name=\"Hiatt-2025\">{{cite magazine |last=Hiatt |first=Brian |title=AI 'Band' the Velvet Sundown Officially Confirm They're AI — and a 'Provocation' |url=https://www.rollingstone.com/music/music-features/ai-band-the-velvet-sundown-confirm-ai-1235379354/ |magazine=Rolling Stone |date=5 July 2025 |access-date=14 August 2025}}</ref> By this time, the band had over one million monthly listeners on Spotify.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2025-07-08 |title= The Velvet Sundown hit 1.1m Spotify listeners – with an honest bio |url=https://musically.com/2025/07/08/the-velvet-sundown-hit-1-1m-spotify-listeners-with-an-honest-bio/|access-date=2025-07-08|website=Music Ally}}</ref> According to a former Spotify employee, the high listener count has two likely causes: firstly, Spotify now accepts payments to boost playlist placement, and secondly, playlists are increasingly selected by algorithms rather than humans.<ref name=\"Hiatt-2025\"/><ref>{{Cite web |last=Farrier |first=David |title=Rolling Stone Gets Hoaxed about a Hoax |url=https://www.webworm.co/p/rollingstonehoax |access-date=2025-07-14 |website=webworm.co |date=13 July 2025 |language=en}}</ref>\n\nIn August 2025, \"Aprimi il culo\", an AI-generated song by the Italian music project [[Cantoscena]], reached number one on Spotify's Viral 50 Italia chart.<ref>{{cite news\n|last=Nasto |first=Vincenzo\n|title=Sesso, stereotipi razziali e sessismo: chi sono i Cantoscena e perché la musica AI è prima in classifica\n|url=https://www.fanpage.it/cultura/sesso-stereotipi-razziali-e-sessismo-chi-sono-i-cantoscena-e-perche-la-musica-ai-e-prima-in-classifica/\n|work=Fanpage.it\n|date=2025-08-08\n|language=it\n}}</ref><ref>{{cite news\n|author=Redazione Web\n|title=La canzone degli anni Sessanta che non è mai esistita: il fenomeno Vera Luna, cantante generata dall’IA\n|url=https://www.deejay.it/articoli/vera-luna-canzone-aprimi-il-cuore/\n|work=Radio Deejay\n|date=2025-08-12\n|language=it\n}}</ref><ref>{{cite news\n|last=Longo |first=Massimiliano\n|title=Che cosa sono tutte queste canzoni a luci Rosse nella Top 50 viral di Spotify? Chi le scrive, canta e pubblica?\n|url=https://www.allmusicitalia.it/approfondimenti-musica-italiana/ma-quale-musica-leggera/top-50-viral-italia-spotify-contenuti-espliciti-artisti-ai.html\n|work=All Music Italia\n|date=2025-08-13\n|language=it\n}}</ref> A later study of the project noted divergent responses to AI disclosure, ranging from dismissive judgments about creative labor and artistic value associated with the label “AI slop” to increased interest among other listeners in the music’s treatment of nostalgia and misinformation.<ref>{{cite journal\n|last=Catanese |first=Matilde\n|date=August 28, 2026\n|title=The year is a dropdown menu: Platformed provenance and the viral afterlives of AI-generated retro music\n|journal=New Media & Society\n|doi=10.1177/14614448261476830\n|doi-access=free\n|pages=13–14\n}}</ref>\n\nAI has also been used to impersonate established musicians and release music on major streaming services under their name without their knowledge, presumably to make money from streaming [[royalties]]. In particular, in August 2025, a number of Americana and folk-rock musicians, including [[Jeff Tweedy]], [[Father John Misty]], and [[Blaze Foley]] (who died in 1989) were impersonated, as well as some established US [[Contemporary Christian music|Christian musicians]] and [[metalcore]] bands. The fake releases all had similar AI-generated [[cover art]] and were credited to the same three record labels. Many listed \"Zyan Maliq Mahardika\" as a songwriter, indicating that the impersonation has a single source. Spotify removed the tracks, stating that they \"violated our policy against impersonating another person or brand\".<ref name=\"Youngs 2025\">{{cite web |last1=Youngs |first1=Ian |last2=Glynn |first2=Paul |title='AI slop': Emily Portman and musicians on the mystery of fraudsters releasing songs in their name |url=https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/clydz8d03dvo |website=bbc.com |date=22 August 2025}}</ref>\n\nIn November 2025, the AI-generated song \"Walk My Walk\" topped the Billboard's country digital song sales chart in the United States with 3,000 sales.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.the-independent.com/arts-entertainment/music/news/breaking-rust-country-ai-singer-billboard-b2863883.html|title=The most downloaded country song in the US right now was made by AI|date=13 November 2025|website=The Independent}}</ref>{{full citation needed|date=November 2025}} The song is by AI artist [[Breaking Rust]], who amassed over 4.5 million [[Spotify]] listeners on their songs. Promotion for \"Walk My Walk\" was accompanied by an AI slop video shared on [[Instagram]] depicting a cowboy walking into the sunset.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Tangermann |first=Victor |date=12 November 2025 |title=Billboard's Top Country Song Is Currently AI Slop |url=https://futurism.com/artificial-intelligence/billboard-top-country-song-ai-slop |access-date=13 November 2025 |work=Futurism}}</ref> In the same month, an AI-generated song named \"[[We Are Charlie Kirk]]\", made in tribute to the [[assassination of Charlie Kirk]] two months earlier, went viral on [[TikTok]] and ranked #1 on Spotify's viral songs chart.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Murray |first=Conor |title=AI-Generated 'We Are Charlie Kirk' Tops Spotify Viral Songs Chart |url=https://www.forbes.com/sites/conormurray/2025/11/20/apparently-ai-generated-song-honoring-charlie-kirk-goes-viral-but-its-far-from-the-first/ |access-date=2025-11-24 |work=Forbes |language=en}}</ref> That month in the [[Netherlands]], the AI-generated [[Far-right politics|far-right]] [[anti-immigrant]] protest song \"[[Wij zeggen nee, nee, nee, tegen een AZC]]\" (We say no, no, no to an asylum shelter) peaked at No. 5 on the [[Dutch Single Top 100]].<ref>{{Cite web |date=2025-11-07 |title=Sophie Straat vindt anti-AZC-lied 'doodeng en gevaarlijk' |url=https://www.rtl.nl/boulevard/artikel/5537721/sophie-straat-vindt-anti-azc-lied-doodeng-en-gevaarlijk |access-date=2025-11-07 |website=RTL.nl |language=nl}}</ref>\n\n==In science==\n\n[[File:AI generated figure published in a Frontiers journal.png|thumb|A now-retracted paper in ''[[Frontiers in Cell and Developmental Biology]]'' included this AI-generated rat with nonsensical anatomy and text.<ref name=ratballs>{{cite q| Q124547290|doi-access=free}}</ref>]]\nGenerative AI has been used to write articles which have been published in both low-quality [[research paper mill]]s and reputable [[scientific journal]]s.<ref name=ratballs/><ref>{{Cite web |last=DeGeurin |first=Mack |date=2024-03-19 |title=AI-generated nonsense is leaking into scientific journals |url=https://www.popsci.com/technology/ai-generated-text-scientific-journals/ |access-date=2025-07-05 |website=Popular Science |language=en-US}}</ref> In 2024, an article containing a generated image of a rat with absurdly large genitals accompanied by nonsensical text and diagrams was retracted by the journal ''[[Frontiers in Cell and Developmental Biology]]'' after drawing attention from scientists on social media.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Mole |first=Beth |date=2024-02-15 |title=Scientists aghast at bizarre AI rat with huge genitals in peer-reviewed article |url=https://arstechnica.com/science/2024/02/scientists-aghast-at-bizarre-ai-rat-with-huge-genitals-in-peer-reviewed-article/ |access-date=2025-07-05 |website=Ars Technica |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=DeGeurin |first=Mack |date=2024-02-16 |title=A ridiculous AI-generated rat penis made it into a peer-reviewed journal |url=https://www.popsci.com/technology/ai-rat-journal/ |access-date=2025-07-05 |website=Popular Science |language=en-US}}</ref> Likewise, in 2025, Nature's ''[[Scientific Reports]]'' retracted the peer-reviewed article \"Bridging the gap: explainable AI for autism diagnosis and parental support with TabPFNMix and SHAP\" because of \"an AI-generated figure\" and other significant errors.<ref>{{Cite journal |last=Jiang |first=Shimei |date=2026-03-03 |title=Retraction Note: Bridging the gap: explainable ai for autism diagnosis and parental support with TabPFNMix and SHAP |journal=Scientific Reports |volume=15 |issue=1 |article-number=43184 |doi=10.1038/s41598-025-31337-y  |doi-access=free|pmid=41350391 |pmc=12680692 |url=https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-025-31337-y}}</ref> [[David Berry (educator)|David Berry]] has used the term ''scholarslop'' to refer to AI-generated administrative discourse in universities or quasi-academic texts.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Berry |first=David |date=2025-10-06 |title=Scholarslop: The Dangers of an Algorithmic Idea of the University |url=https://stunlaw.blogspot.com/2025/10/the-coming-threat-of-algorithmic-idea.html |access-date=2026-02-05 |website=Stunlaw}}</ref><ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Macgilchrist |first1=Felicitas |last2=Pangrazio |first2=Luci |last3=Potter |first3=John |last4=Williamson |first4=Ben |date=2025-10-02 |title=Future challenges for critical research on educational technologies |url=https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/17439884.2025.2589752 |journal=Learning, Media and Technology |language=en |volume=50 |issue=4 |pages=411–416 |doi=10.1080/17439884.2025.2589752 |issn=1743-9884}}</ref> Glen Berman argues that AI-generated articles being published in scholarly journals are an \"[[epistemology|epistemic]] [[carcinogen]]\" that will pose a major risk to the knowledge ecosystem.<ref>{{Cite journal |last=Berman |first=Glen |date=2025-08-13 |title=GenAI is an epistemic carcinogen |url=https://link.springer.com/10.1007/s00146-025-02537-x |journal=AI & Society |volume=41 |issue=2 |pages=1353–1355 |language=en |doi=10.1007/s00146-025-02537-x |issn=0951-5666}}</ref>\n\n== In technology ==\n{{See also|Vibe coding}}\nIn January 2026, the open-source command-line utility [[cURL]] ended its [[bug bounty program]] through [[HackerOne]] after receiving a large number of false vulnerability reports.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Abrams |first=Lawrence |title=Curl ending bug bounty program after flood of AI slop reports |url=https://www.bleepingcomputer.com/news/security/curl-ending-bug-bounty-program-after-flood-of-ai-slop-reports/ |access-date=2026-01-23 |website=BleepingComputer}}</ref> In an announcement on its official [[GitHub]] page, the project stated that rewards would no longer be offered for reported vulnerabilities. [[Daniel Stenberg]], founder and lead developer of cURL, shared examples of fake, AI-generated bug reports submitted through the program, including one that claimed a critical vulnerability that did not actually exist. Many of these reports referenced nonexistent [[changelog]]s, included code snippets that did not match real function signatures, or were the result of simple user errors. Stenberg explained that the influx of AI-generated reports placed a significant burden on cURL's security team and that ending the program was an \"attempt to reduce the noise.\"<ref>{{Cite news |last=Sharwood |first=Simon |title=Curl shutters bug bounty program to stop AI slop |url=https://www.theregister.com/2026/01/21/curl_ends_bug_bounty/ |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260122062713/https://www.theregister.com/2026/01/21/curl_ends_bug_bounty/ |archive-date=22 January 2026 |access-date=2026-01-23 |url-status=live }}</ref>\n\n==In business==\nA ''[[Harvard Business Review]]'' study, done in conjunction with [[Stanford University]] and [[BetterUp]], found that employees were using AI tools to create low-effort \"workslop\" that created more work for their colleagues.<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Niederhoffer |first1=Kate |last2=Kellerman |first2=Gabriella Rosen |last3=Lee |first3=Angela |last4=Liebscher |first4=Alex |last5=Rapuano |first5=Kristina |last6=Hancock |first6=Jeffrey T. |title=AI-Generated \"Workslop\" Is Destroying Productivity |url=https://hbr.org/2025/09/ai-generated-workslop-is-destroying-productivity |journal=Harvard Business Review |date=22 September 2025 }}</ref> Within the timeframe of the study, it was found that 40% of participating employees received some form of \"workslop\", with each incident taking an average of two hours to resolve.<ref name=\"BetterUp\"/> BetterUp defines workslop as \"AI-generated content that looks good, but lacks substance\".<ref name=\"BetterUp\">{{cite web |title=Workslop |url=https://www.betterup.com/workslop |website=BetterUp}}</ref> The study appears to be the first instance of \"workslop\" and also uses \"workslopped\" as a verb.<ref>{{cite news |last=Peck |first=Emily |title=AI \"workslop\" sabotages productivity, study finds |url=https://www.axios.com/2025/09/24/ai-workslop-workplace-efficiency-study |work=Axios}}</ref> In a subsequent study, ''Harvard Business Review'' coined the word \"trendslop\", referring to the \"propensity for AI to opt for buzzy ideas over reasoned solutions.\"<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Romasanta |first1=Angelo |last2=Thomas |first2=Llewellyn D. W. |last3=Levina |first3=Natalia |title=Researchers Asked LLMs for Strategic Advice. They Got \"Trendslop\" in Return. |url=https://hbr.org/2026/03/researchers-asked-llms-for-strategic-advice-they-got-trendslop-in-return |journal=Harvard Business Review |date=16 March 2026 }}</ref> \n\nIn January 2026, [[Microsoft]] CEO [[Satya Nadella]] urged that people stop using the term \"slop\" and instead accept AI as the \"new equilibrium\" of human nature.<ref name=\"microslop\">{{cite news|url=https://futurism.com/artificial-intelligence/microsoft-satya-nadella-ai-slop|title=Microsoft CEO Begs Users to Stop Calling It “Slop”|newspaper=Futurism|first=Joe|last=Wilkins|date=January 4, 2026|url-status=live}}</ref> The resulting public backlash was described as a [[Streisand effect]], and the term \"Microslop\" rose in popularity.<ref name=\"microslop2\">{{cite news|url=https://futurism.com/artificial-intelligence/video-microsoft-ruining-windows-ai|title=“Microslop”: Infuriating Video Sums Up How Microsoft Is Ruining Windows With AI|newspaper=[[Futurism (website)|Futurism]]|first=Frank|last=Landymore|date=January 7, 2026|url-status=live}}</ref><ref name=\"microslop3\">{{cite news|url=https://www.yahoo.com/news/articles/microslop-trends-backlash-microsofts-ai-160628586.html|title=\"Microslop\" trends in backlash to Microsoft's AI obsession|newspaper=[[Yahoo News]]|first=Jez|last=Corden|date=January 3, 2026|url-status=live}}</ref> In March 2026, Microsoft banned the word \"Microslop\" on a [[Discord]] server dedicated to its [[Microsoft Copilot|Copilot]] AI tool, drawing further attention to the term.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2026-03-02 |title=Microsoft Effort to Ban 'Microslop' on Copilot Discord Didn't Go As Planned |url=https://au.pcmag.com/ai/116268/microsoft-effort-to-ban-microslop-on-copilot-discord-didnt-go-as-planned |access-date=2026-03-16 |website=PCMag Australia |language=en-au|last=Martindale|first=John}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |date=2026-03-02 |title=The term 'Microslop' has overrun the Microsoft Copilot Discord server, and attempts to moderate it have gone badly |url=https://www.pcgamer.com/software/ai/microsoft-banned-the-word-microslop-in-its-copilot-discord-server-then-began-restricting-access-after-users-started-posting-microsl0p-and-other-funnies/ |access-date=2026-03-16 |last=Edser|work=PC Gamer |language=en|first=Andy}}</ref>\n\n== In sports ==\nAn AI-generated scene of [[Sabrina Impacciatore]] skating and skiing across historic host cities at the [[2026 Winter Olympics opening ceremony]] was criticized for having replaced animators,<ref>{{Cite news|last=Patton |first=Tess |date=February 6, 2026 |title=Winter Olympics Viewers Slam 'AI Slop' in Opening Ceremony Flashback Sequence |url=https://www.thewrap.com/media-platforms/tv/winter-olympics-opening-ceremony-ai-slop-sabrina-impacciatore/ |work=[[TheWrap]]}}</ref> as well as a poor depiction of the [[Olympic rings]] that violated the Olympic brand guidelines.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2026-02-09 |title=Official Olympic Account Accused Of Violating Its Own Guideline With Controversial Post |url=https://www.sportbible.com/athletics/winter-olympics-social-media-post-ai-guideline-718411-20260209 |access-date=2026-02-20 |website=SPORTbible |language=en}}</ref> The ice dancers [[Kateřina Mrázková]] and [[Daniel Mrázek]] were also criticized for dancing to an AI-generated song that combined AC/DC's \"[[Thunderstruck]]\" with the AI-composed track \"One Two\", imitating [[Bon Jovi]]'s melody.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Pahwa |first=Nitish |date=2026-02-13 |title=Welcome to the First Sloplympics |url=https://slate.com/culture/2026/02/ai-slop-at-the-2026-olympics.html |access-date=2026-02-20 |work=[[Slate (magazine)|Slate]] |language=en-US}}</ref> The professional wrestling company [[WWE]] has also faced heavy criticism for their usage of AI in promotional materials including in an incident where [[AAA Mega Championship|AAA Mega Champion]] [[Dominik Mysterio]] was depicted wearing the championship belt of rival promotion, [[All Elite Wrestling]].<ref>{{Cite web |last=read |first=Kebin Antony·2 min |date=2026-01-19 |title=WWE's Dominik Mysterio Advertised As AEW Champion Thanks To AI Screwup |url=https://sports.yahoo.com/articles/wwe-dominik-mysterio-advertised-aew-232238488.html |access-date=2026-08-06 |website=Yahoo Sports |language=en-US}}</ref>\n\n==See also==\n{{div col|colwidth=30em}}\n*{{annotated link|Artificial intelligence in spirituality}}\n*{{annotated link|AI boom}}\n*{{annotated link|AI bubble}}\n*{{Annotated link|Brain rot}}\n*{{annotated link|Clanker}}\n*{{annotated link|Dead Internet theory}}\n*{{annotated link|Elsagate}}\n*{{annotated link|Enshittification}}\n*{{annotated link|Generative AI pornography}}\n*{{annotated link|Generative engine optimization}}\n*{{annotated link|Goyslop}}\n*{{annotated link|Hallucination (artificial intelligence)}}\n*{{annotated link|Low culture}}\n*{{annotated link|Remix culture}}\n*{{annotated link|Search engine optimization}}\n*{{annotated link|Slopaganda}}\n*{{Annotated link|Sludge content}}\n*{{annotated link|Uncanny valley}}\n*{{annotated link|Your AI Slop Bores Me}}\n{{div col end}}\n\n==References==\n{{reflist}}\n\n\n\n{{Generative AI}}\n{{Artificial intelligence navbox|state=collapsed}}\n{{2020s slang|state=collapsed}}\n{{Subject bar|auto=yes|wikt=AI slop|portal1=2020s|portal2=Art|portal3=Internet|portal4=Technology}}\n\n[[Category:2020s neologisms]]\n[[Category:2020s slang]]\n[[Category:AI risk]]\n[[Category:Articles containing video clips]]\n[[Category:AI art]]\n[[Category:Criticism of artificial intelligence]]\n[[Category:Generative AI]]\n[[Category:Internet memes introduced in the 2020s]]\n[[Category:Pejorative terms related to technology]]\n[[Category:Works created using generative AI]]\n[[Category:2020s in artificial intelligence]]"}}}],"contentmodel":"wikitext","pagelanguage":"en","pagelanguagehtmlcode":"en","pagelanguagedir":"ltr","touched":"2026-11-19T05:37:24Z","lastrevid":1384670855,"length":91339}]}}